        salesDrivers: ["**The ECH and CPVC doubling** (about ₹650cr, from its own cash): promised for Q2 FY27, now due 'within a couple of months' of late September, ramping from 15–20% to about 75–80% use in FY28.", "**A ₹600cr epoxy-resin and multipurpose plant** approved in July 2026 for H2 FY28, funded 60% with debt: peak sales of ₹1,300–1,500cr, or about ₹2.2–2.5 of sales per rupee spent against about ₹1.2 for its existing plants. Half the enlarged ECH output will feed it.", "**Management's targets**: revenue and profit up 15–20% a year for three to five years, about ₹2,900cr of revenue in FY27 and ₹5,000cr by FY31, at about 20% return on capital on the new projects. Capex is about ₹400cr in each of FY27 and FY28.", "**Caustic-soda and ECH prices**: the caustic-plus-chlorine unit price rose from about ₹30,000 a tonne to ₹35,000–36,000 in Q1 on the war and was back to ₹31,000–32,000 by late July; ECH fell from ₹200–210 a kilo to ₹180–190.", "**CPVC demand and duties**: the CPVC price follows PVC with a lag, and Epigral, DCW and Lubrizol asked in September 2026 for action against Chinese resin routed through third countries."],
        inputs: "**Salt and electricity** make the caustic soda and chlorine; **glycerine** (from palm-oil biodiesel) goes into ECH; **PVC** is bought in and chlorinated into CPVC; **methanol** goes into chloromethanes and **toluene** into chlorotoluenes. Coal fuels its 132 MW power plant. Materials were 57% of sales in FY26; power, fuel and plant costs 13%.",
        costEffect: "**Partly passed on, with a lag.** Caustic soda and ECH sell at market prices that move with imports, and CPVC follows the PVC price 'not immediately, but gradually', so a falling PVC market leaves it holding dear stock. Its own power shields it from grid-power costs, which rose about 15% after the war, but not from coal. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 6 points (22% to 16%). **What happened:** there is no single price index for this group, because what matters is the spread between salt, power, glycerine and PVC on one side and caustic, ECH and CPVC prices on the other. The quarterly margin went from 27% in Q1 FY26 to 22%, then 17% in Q3 FY26 (a stock loss on PVC in a falling market), back to 23% in Q4 and 25% in Q1 FY27. **Q1 FY27 was helped by the war**: caustic prices rose 30–35%, and they were already easing by late July.",
