        costEffect: "**Not protected: the spread is set by imports and policy.** VCM is bought in dollars months ahead, while PVC sells at import parity, so a VCM spike or a duty waiver lands straight on the margin; parcels booked in March at war prices led to ₹150cr of provisions in Q4 FY26 and ₹120cr more in Q1 FY27. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 6 points (5% to about −1%). **What happened:** there is no single price index for this group. The quarterly margin was 2% and 4% in Q1–Q2 FY26, −7% in Q3 as PVC prices fell, 15% in Q4 (with a ₹150cr provision for loss-making VCM contracts booked separately), then **−10% in Q1 FY27** as VCM bought at war prices met a waived PVC duty. **Unlike most of this group, the war hurt it**; management expects 'reasonable performance from Q3'.",
